          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Does \"with\" require the two terms to sit next to each other in the documentation, or is it an index convention?",
          "answer": "**Short answer:** The \"with\" convention is an index instruction. It is not a rule that the two terms must sit next to each other in the record.\n\n**What the corpus says:**\n- Where the Alphabetic Index links two conditions with \"with,\" the classification reads this as \"associated with\" or \"due to.\" It presumes that relationship without requiring the record to state it explicitly. (*Coding type 2 diabetes: E11*, and *ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection*.)\n- The presumption applies only to pairings the index actually lists. It is overridden when the documentation says the two conditions are unrelated. (*ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection*.)\n\n**What the corpus does not say:** It does not address whether the two terms must appear near each other in a note. The retrieved passages describe the index's linking as the source of the presumption, not any wording or position requirement in the documentation. I can't say more about adjacency from these sources.\n\nCoding decisions are made by trained coders working from the full record, so questions about a specific chart should go to them.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
